--- Rosetta Code: Exponentiation order
--- https://rosettacode.org/wiki/Exponentiation_order
--- Crow's power operator is ^ (never a bit operation: bitxor is a function of `use bits`), and
--- .^ is the same power item by item over a list of numbers. Both are right-associative, as in
--- mathematics: a ^ b ^ c is a ^ (b ^ c).

log['5 ^ 3 ^ 2   = `5 ^ 3 ^ 2`']
log['(5 ^ 3) ^ 2 = `(5 ^ 3) ^ 2`']
log['5 ^ (3 ^ 2) = `5 ^ (3 ^ 2)`']
log['']
log['The same order elsewhere:']
log['5.0 ^ 3.0 ^ 2.0           = `fmt[5.0 ^ 3.0 ^ 2.0, decimals = 1]`  (floats)']
log['decimal[5] ^ 3 ^ 2        = `decimal[5] ^ 3 ^ 2`  (an exact decimal)']
log['fraction[1, 5] ^ 3 ^ 2    = `fraction[1, 5] ^ 3 ^ 2`  (an exact fraction)']
log['2 ^ 3 ^ 4                 = `2 ^ 3 ^ 4`  (2 ^ 81: ints grow)']
log['2 ^ 2 ^ 2 ^ 2             = `2 ^ 2 ^ 2 ^ 2`']
log['-5 ^ 3 ^ 2                = `-5 ^ 3 ^ 2`  (a power binds tighter than a minus)']
log['5.0 ^ -3 ^ 2              = `5.0 ^ -3 ^ 2`  (5 ^ -(3 ^ 2): an exponent may carry a sign)']
log['[5, 6, 7] .^ 3 ^ 2        = `[5, 6, 7] .^ 3 ^ 2`']
log['([5, 6, 7] .^ 3) .^ 2     = `([5, 6, 7] .^ 3) .^ 2`']
